<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anna of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1030–after 1075</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Anne of Kiev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> who became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as the wife of Henry I, whom she married in 1051. She was the mother of Philip I and acted with him in government during his minority after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s death in 1060. Her later marriage to Count Ralph of Valois caused a scandal. Her marriage is a sign of the standing of the Rurikid dynasty in eleventh-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anna-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Anna of Kiev" width="240" height="502" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AnnaKyjev.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
